Second Part
Form and Mechanism of Services for Violence Against Women and Children Article 21 (1) In the context of providing protection services to women and children from acts of violence, the Regional Government establishes the Regional Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children (UPTD PPA), Integrated Referral Service System (SLRT), Task Force on Violence Against Women/Children (Tim KTP/A) in community health centers, Hospitals, schools, Islamic Boarding Schools, Women-Friendly and Child-Caring Villages (DRPPA).
(2) The Regional Government through UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1), functions to implement services:
a. Public complaints;
b. Victim outreach;
c. Case management;
d. Temporary shelter;
e. Mediation;
f. Victim assistance; and
g. Victim repatriation.
(3) Further provisions regarding the organization and working procedures of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation. (4) To assist in the protection of women and children, the Regional Government establishes:
a. Task Force for the Prevention and Handling of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking;
b. Task Force for the Prevention of Child Marriage;
c. Task Force for Child-Friendly Regency;
d. Regional Action Committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor or Worst Forms of Child Labor (BPTA); e. Regional Children's Forum (FAD);
f. Community-Based Integrated Child Protection (PATBM) PENA BISMA;
g. Domestic Violence Forum (KDRT);
h. Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children (Satgas PPA); i. Women-Friendly and Child-Caring Villages (DRPPA);
j. TIM KTP/A in Hospitals, Community health centers, schools, Islamic Boarding Schools.
(5) Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties, and functions of the task forces as referred to in paragraph (4) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 25
(1) In order to achieve the objectives of protection of women and children, cooperation may be conducted with:
a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regency/City Governments;
d. Non-Governmental Institutions;
e. Community;
f. Higher Education Institutions;
g. Business Sector;
h. Mass Media;
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. Exchange of data and information;
b. Recovery of victims of violence;
c. Repatriation and social reintegration;
d. Provision of evidence and witnesses; and
e. Followed up in accordance with applicable law.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be set forth in the form of a Cooperation Agreement in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER IV
FORMS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN Article 4 Forms of violence against women and children, among others: a. Physical violence;
b. Psychological violence;
c. Sexual violence;
d. Neglect;
e. Exploitation;
f. Other violence, namely:
1) Victims of pornography;
2) Victims of disaster and conflict;
3) Victims of narcotics;
4) Victims infected with HIV/AIDS;
5) Victims of terrorism;
6) Victims of Stigmatization;
7) Worst Forms of Child Labor (WFCL);
8) Street children and deviant behavior;
9) Persons with disabilities and/or;
10) Children in Need of Special Protection and others.

CHAPTER V
RIGHTS OF VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN Article 11 Women and children victims of acts of violence receive the following rights:
a. Right to have their dignity and worth as human beings respected; b. Right to recovery;
c. Right to determine their own decisions;
d. Right to obtain information;
e. Right to confidentiality;
f. Right to social rehabilitation and social reintegration;
g. Right to complaint handling that is appropriate, comfortable, and according to needs;
h. Right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
i. Right to assistance; and
j. To receive continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage. Article 12 Child victims of acts of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 11, also receive special rights, as follows: a. Right to survival, growth, and development; b. Right to basic population services;
c. Right to equal protection;
d. Right to be free from various stigma; and
e. Right to obtain freedom.

